# Package set for the RECOVERY slot (p7).
#
# p7 is 450 MiB against the A/B slots' 850 MiB, and the A/B image already uses
# 669 MiB. So this list is not "the same thing, smaller" -- it is only what is
# needed to GET IN and REPAIR A SLOT, and nothing whatsoever else. Every entry
# below earns its bytes against that single job.
#
# The job, concretely:
#   1. come up on the network unattended        (systemd-networkd, resolved)
#   2. let someone in                           (openssh-server, + tailscale
#                                                installed from the upstream
#                                                tarball, not a package)
#   3. fetch an image                           (curl, ca-certificates)
#   4. write and verify a slot                  (coreutils, gzip, e2fsprogs)
#   5. move the boot pointer                    (libubootenv-tool)
#
# ONE DELIBERATE EXCEPTION to "nothing whatsoever else": zsh, below. It is a
# comfort package by the rule this list is written to, and it is here anyway
# because the argument for it is strongest exactly where the rule is strictest
# -- see its entry. The cost was measured, not estimated, and it is recorded
# there. Every OTHER comfort package stays out; the exception is not a new
# policy.

systemd-sysv
udev
dbus
busybox
linux-base

# The U-Boot environment. Without this the recovery slot can write a slot but
# cannot make the box boot it, which is half a repair.
libubootenv-tool

# Network, unattended.
systemd-resolved
systemd-timesyncd
iproute2
iputils-ping
ca-certificates
curl

# Access.
openssh-server
openssh-client

# Repair.
e2fsprogs
fdisk
util-linux
mmc-utils

# Enough of an editor to fix a config by hand over serial at 115200 baud.
# nano rather than vim: a tenth of the size, and nobody needs modal editing
# while the box is down.
nano

# The interactive shell, and the exception noted at the top of this file.
#
# COST. Free space is measured, from the recovery image built 2026-08-03
# (rootfs/out/omni-recovery.ext4: 21,477 free blocks x 4096). The package sizes
# are Debian trixie/arm64 Installed-Size, i.e. BEFORE the build's --slim pass
# strips /usr/share/{doc,man,info,locale} -- so they are an upper bound and the
# free-space figure is a lower bound:
#
#   free before      21,477 blocks x 4096 = 83.9 MiB
#   zsh                                     4.0 MiB
#   zsh-common (hard Depends)              16.0 MiB   <- almost all of it
#   oh-my-zsh, pruned to 3 plugins          0.9 MiB   (measured, not estimated)
#   free after                            >=63 MiB
#
# Recommends are off, so zsh-doc and the pcre/gdbm module libraries stay out.
# The 11 MB of upstream plugins are deleted at build time, which is what makes
# oh-my-zsh cost 0.9 MiB here instead of 12.
#
# WHY IT EARNS ITS BYTES ON THE PARTITION THAT ALLOWS NOTHING:
#
#   The prompt says which slot you are on. The theme reads root= out of
#   /proc/cmdline once at shell start and prints a red [RECOVERY] on every
#   line. /etc/update-motd.d/00-omni-recovery already says so at login, and
#   then it scrolls off the top of a 115200 baud console and you spend the
#   next hour on a slot with no overlay, wondering why your changes keep
#   disappearing. Recovery is used rarely, under pressure, by someone who was
#   doing something else ten minutes ago -- the conditions under which
#   "which box am I on" errors actually happen.
#
#   Up-arrow history search. Recovery sessions are a handful of long
#   omni-flash.sh command lines with a URL, a sha256 and a byte count in them,
#   retyped after each failure. On a serial line with no scrollback and no
#   mouse, recalling one by typing three characters is not a luxury.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: /bin/sh stays dash here too. The recovery /init is
# '#!/bin/sh', omni-flash.sh is '#!/bin/sh', and the build fails if /bin/sh
# ever resolves to zsh. Only root's INTERACTIVE login shell changes.
#
# If p7 ever runs out of room, this is the first thing to drop -- build with
# --no-zsh, which leaves root on the Debian default and changes nothing else.
zsh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT INSTALLED (present in the A/B image, omitted here)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   initramfs-tools   The recovery slot boots with ramdisk_addr_r cleared and
#                     init=/init -- there is no initrd in this boot path at all,
#                     so building one wastes ~30 MiB and a lot of build time.
#                     This is the single biggest saving in the list.
#   vim, btop, jq     comfort, not repair. (zsh IS installed and is also a
#                     comfort package; see its entry above for why that one
#                     exception was made and what it cost. These three were
#                     re-examined at the same time and still do not earn it:
#                     nano covers editing, /proc covers what btop shows, and
#                     nothing in the recovery path parses JSON.)
#   tcpdump, iperf3   diagnosis of a WORKING box; this slot exists for a broken
#   fio               one, and you can install them at runtime if you must.
#   wireguard-tools   the recovery path out is ssh or tailscale, not a tunnel
#   nftables/iptables that has to be configured first.
